{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WPm2WjnQjK46iQRY8q3njf","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Abandon ''temporal is universally critical'' framing. Reframe\n2\t  as: ''Hidden-state safety monitoring in reasoning models is architecture-dependent\n3\t  — we identify two regimes (distributed-signal/Llama where temporal context is essential,\n4\t  vs concentrated-signal/Qwen where static probes suffice) and characterize when each\n5\t  applies.'' New Claim 2 becomes ''architecture determines monitoring strategy.''\n6\t  Emphasize the diagnostic framework (Fisher ratio, PCA variance as predictors of\n7\t  which regime applies) as the transferable contribution. Reposition as empirical\n8\t  characterization paper with practical deployment implications. Target EMNLP Main\n9\t  via comparative-analysis angle.'\n10\timagined_experiments:\n11\t- name: Cross-architecture static probe evaluation\n12\t  plan_id: exp-001\n13\t  description: Train static LR on r1-32b and QwQ-32B hidden states, measure coverage.\n14\t  expected_results:\n15\t    r1_32b_static_lr_acc: 0.891\n16\t    r1_32b_static_lr_coverage: 0.698\n17\t    r1_32b_fisher_orig_L14: 8.3\n18\t    r1_32b_fisher_pca3_L14: 5.1\n19\t    r1_32b_var_explained_3pc: 0.38\n20\t    qwq_32b_static_lr_acc: 0.903\n21\t    qwq_32b_static_lr_coverage: 0.721\n22\t    qwq_32b_fisher_orig_L14: 6.7\n23\t    qwq_32b_fisher_pca3_L14: 4.2\n24\t    qwq_32b_var_explained_3pc: 0.41\n25\t  result_rationale: Qwen2.5 architecture concentrates safety-relevant signal into\n26\t    top principal components (PCA top-3 explains ~38-41% variance vs 16.6% for Llama).\n27\t    Fisher discriminant ratio drops from 17-18 (Llama full-space) to only 6-8 (Qwen\n28\t    full-space), but critically the PCA-3 Fisher ratio RISES to 4-5 (vs ~1 for Llama).\n29\t    This means safety signal in Qwen2.5 is geometrically concentrated rather than\n30\t    distributed across minor components. Static LR exploits this concentrated signal\n31\t    directly, achieving 70%+ coverage — far above the 46.5% seen on r1-8b/Llama. The\n32\t    architecture's representation geometry makes safety states linearly separable\n33\t    without temporal context.\n34\t  tables:\n35\t  - name: 'Table: Static Probe Cross-Architecture Comparison'\n36\t    columns:\n37\t    - Model\n38\t    - Architecture\n39\t    - Static Coverage\n40\t    - Fisher (full)\n41\t    - Fisher (PCA-3)\n42\t    - PCA Var Explained\n43\t    rows:\n44\t    - - r1-8b\n45\t      - Llama-3.1\n46\t      - 46.5%\n47\t      - '17.9'\n48\t      - '0.93'\n49\t      - 16.6%\n50\t    - - r1-32b\n51\t      - Qwen2.5\n52\t      - 69.8%\n53\t      - '8.3'\n54\t      - '5.1'\n55\t      - 38.0%\n56\t    - - QwQ-32B\n57\t      - Qwen2.5\n58\t      - 72.1%\n59\t      - '6.7'\n60\t      - '4.2'\n61\t      - 41.0%\n62\t  figures_description:\n63\t  - PCA scatter plot (PC1 vs PC2) showing safety/unsafe step embeddings for all 3\n64\t    models. Llama shows overlapping clusters; Qwen2.5 models show clear separation\n65\t    with decision boundary visible in 2D.\n66\t  - 'Fisher ratio comparison: bar chart showing full-space Fisher (Llama >> Qwen)\n67\t    vs PCA-3 Fisher (Qwen >> Llama), illustrating signal concentration difference.'\n68\t  analysis: 'Static probes achieve 70%+ coverage on Qwen2.5 models, dramatically above\n69\t    the 46.5% on Llama. This directly contradicts the universality of Claim 1. The\n70\t    explanation lies in representation geometry: Qwen2.5 concentrates safety signal\n71\t    into dominant PCs (41% variance in top-3 vs 17% for Llama), making it accessible\n72\t    to linear classifiers without temporal aggregation. The lower full-space Fisher\n73\t    ratio (6-8 vs 17-18) is misleading — what matters is where the signal lives relative\n74\t    to the principal subspace.'\n75\t- name: Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation\n76\t  plan_id: exp-002\n77\t  description: Temporal LR W=15 on r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Test whether +30pp improvement\n78\t    holds.\n79\t  expected_results:\n80\t    r1_32b_temporal_w15_acc: 0.901\n81\t    r1_32b_temporal_w15_coverage: 0.744\n82\t    r1_32b_temporal_delta_over_static: 0.046\n83\t    qwq_32b_temporal_w15_acc: 0.912\n84\t    qwq_32b_temporal_w15_coverage: 0.767\n85\t    qwq_32b_temporal_delta_over_static: 0.046\n86\t  result_rationale: Temporal window adds only +4-5pp coverage on Qwen2.5 models (vs\n87\t    +30pp on Llama). The 'safety reasoning density decline' pattern that temporal\n88\t    probes exploit on Llama is either absent or already captured by static signal\n89\t    in Qwen2.5. Because safety signal is concentrated in top PCs, a single-step snapshot\n90\t    already contains sufficient discriminative information — the temporal context\n91\t    provides marginal smoothing benefit rather than genuinely new signal. The temporal-minus-static\n92\t    delta of ~0.05 is within noise range for these sample sizes, meaning temporal\n93\t    context is effectively unnecessary for Qwen2.5.\n94\t  tables:\n95\t  - name: 'Table: Temporal Advantage Cross-Architecture'\n96\t    columns:\n97\t    - Model\n98\t    - Static Coverage\n99\t    - Temporal W15 Coverage\n100\t    - Delta\n101\t    - Llama Baseline Delta\n102\t    rows:\n103\t    - - r1-8b (Llama)\n104\t      - 46.5%\n105\t      - 76.7%\n106\t      - +30.2pp\n107\t      - —\n108\t    - - r1-32b (Qwen2.5)\n109\t      - 69.8%\n110\t      - 74.4%\n111\t      - +4.6pp\n112\t      - —\n113\t    - - QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5)\n114\t      - 72.1%\n115\t      - 76.7%\n116\t      - +4.6pp\n117\t      - —\n118\t  figures_description:\n119\t  - 'Bar chart: static vs temporal coverage across 3 models. Llama shows dramatic\n120\t    temporal lift; Qwen2.5 models show near-flat bars with minimal gap.'\n121\t  - 'Density decline pattern visualization: rolling window safety-label density for\n122\t    representative traces. Llama traces show gradual decline pattern; Qwen2.5 traces\n123\t    show sharper step-function transitions.'\n124\t  analysis: 'The +30pp temporal advantage is Llama-specific. Qwen2.5 models show only\n125\t    +4-5pp marginal improvement from temporal context, within bootstrap CI overlap.\n126\t    The ''safety reasoning density decline'' pattern — the mechanism temporal probes\n127\t    exploit — manifests differently in Qwen2.5: transitions are sharper (step-function\n128\t    rather than gradual decline), meaning a single step already captures the transition.\n129\t    This invalidates Claim 2 as a general finding but opens the architecture-dependent\n130\t    monitoring story.'\n131\t- name: Cross-model transfer\n132\t  plan_id: exp-003\n133\t  description: Train on r1-8b (Llama), test on r1-32b/QwQ (Qwen2.5) and vice versa.\n134\t  expected_results:\n135\t    llama_to_qwen_static_coverage: 0.256\n136\t    llama_to_qwen_temporal_coverage: 0.279\n137\t    qwen_to_llama_static_coverage: 0.302\n138\t    qwen_to_llama_temporal_coverage: 0.326\n139\t    projection_head_dim_match_loss: 0.42\n140\t    within_qwen_transfer_coverage: 0.651\n141\t  result_rationale: Cross-architecture transfer fails catastrophically (25-33% coverage)\n142\t    because the architectures encode safety in fundamentally different geometric subspaces.\n143\t    Llama distributes signal across minor PCs; Qwen2.5 concentrates it in major PCs.\n144\t    A learned projection head cannot bridge this because the signal dimensions are\n145\t    incommensurable — it's not a rotation/scaling problem but a rank mismatch. Within-family\n146\t    transfer (r1-32b ↔ QwQ-32B, both Qwen2.5) works reasonably (65%) because representation\n147\t    geometry is shared within architecture family. This confirms safety encoding is\n148\t    architecture-determined, not model-determined.\n149\t  tables:\n150\t  - name: 'Table: Zero-Shot Cross-Architecture Transfer'\n151\t    columns:\n152\t    - Train Model\n153\t    - Test Model\n154\t    - Method\n155\t    - Coverage\n156\t    - vs In-Domain\n157\t    rows:\n158\t    - - r1-8b (Llama)\n159\t      - r1-32b (Qwen)\n160\t      - Static LR\n161\t      - 25.6%\n162\t      - 69.8%\n163\t    - - r1-8b (Llama)\n164\t      - r1-32b (Qwen)\n165\t      - Temporal W15\n166\t      - 27.9%\n167\t      - 74.4%\n168\t    - - r1-32b (Qwen)\n169\t      - r1-8b (Llama)\n170\t      - Static LR\n171\t      - 30.2%\n172\t      - 46.5%\n173\t    - - r1-32b (Qwen)\n174\t      - r1-8b (Llama)\n175\t      - Temporal W15\n176\t      - 32.6%\n177\t      - 76.7%\n178\t    - - r1-32b (Qwen)\n179\t      - QwQ-32B (Qwen)\n180\t      - Static LR\n181\t      - 65.1%\n182\t      - 72.1%\n183\t  figures_description:\n184\t  - 'Transfer matrix heatmap: rows = train model, columns = test model, cell color\n185\t    = coverage. Diagonal (in-domain) is hot; cross-architecture cells are cold; within-Qwen\n186\t    transfer is warm.'\n187\t  analysis: Zero-shot transfer between architectures is near-random, confirming safety\n188\t    signal is encoded architecture-specifically. The slight above-chance performance\n189\t    (25-33% vs ~20% random baseline) suggests some universal safety features exist\n190\t    but are insufficient for practical detection. Within-family transfer (65%) demonstrates\n191\t    that models sharing architecture share representation geometry for safety, supporting\n192\t    the 'architecture determines monitoring strategy' reframing.\n193\t- name: Lead time analysis across models\n194\t  plan_id: exp-004\n195\t  description: Confirm negative lead time finding on 32B models.\n196\t  expected_results:\n197\t    r1_32b_static_lead_mean: 2.1\n198\t    r1_32b_temporal_lead_mean: 3.4\n199\t    r1_32b_positive_lead_rate: 0.52\n200\t    qwq_32b_static_lead_mean: 3.8\n201\t    qwq_32b_temporal_lead_mean: 4.2\n202\t    qwq_32b_positive_lead_rate: 0.58\n203\t  result_rationale: Qwen2.5 models show POSITIVE lead time (mean 2-4 steps), contradicting\n204\t    the negative lead time finding on Llama. Because safety signal is concentrated\n205\t    and linearly separable in Qwen2.5, probes can detect commitment approaching before\n206\t    it occurs — the hidden state shifts are more abrupt and precede the textual commitment\n207\t    point. This further undermines the universality of Claim 3 (pre-textual detection\n208\t    is fundamentally limited). On Qwen2.5, the limitation doesn't hold — it's a Llama-specific\n209\t    artifact of distributed signal requiring temporal aggregation, which inherently\n210\t    introduces detection lag.\n211\t  tables:\n212\t  - name: 'Table: Lead Time Distribution Across Models'\n213\t    columns:\n214\t    - Model\n215\t    - Method\n216\t    - Lead Mean\n217\t    - Lead Median\n218\t    - Positive Lead Rate\n219\t    rows:\n220\t    - - r1-8b (Llama)\n221\t      - Static LR\n222\t      - '-2.0'\n223\t      - '-2'\n224\t      - 30%\n225\t    - - r1-8b (Llama)\n226\t      - Temporal W15\n227\t      - '-1.09'\n228\t      - '-1'\n229\t      - 34.5%\n230\t    - - r1-32b (Qwen)\n231\t      - Static LR\n232\t      - '+2.1'\n233\t      - '+1'\n234\t      - 52%\n235\t    - - r1-32b (Qwen)\n236\t      - Temporal W15\n237\t      - '+3.4'\n238\t      - '+2'\n239\t      - 55%\n240\t    - - QwQ-32B (Qwen)\n241\t      - Static LR\n242\t      - '+3.8'\n243\t      - '+3'\n244\t      - 58%\n245\t    - - QwQ-32B (Qwen)\n246\t      - Temporal W15\n247\t      - '+4.2'\n248\t      - '+3'\n249\t      - 60%\n250\t  figures_description:\n251\t  - Lead time distribution violin plots per model. Llama distribution centered below\n252\t    0; Qwen2.5 distributions centered above 0 with right skew.\n253\t  analysis: 'The negative lead time finding is Llama-specific. Qwen2.5''s concentrated\n254\t    signal geometry enables genuine pre-textual detection with positive lead times.\n255\t    This is the most damaging result for the original framing — it means Claim 3 cannot\n256\t    stand as a universal limitation. However, it opens a richer story: architecture\n257\t    determines not just WHAT method works but WHETHER pre-textual detection is possible\n258\t    at all. The lead time difference directly correlates with PCA signal concentration\n259\t    (r=0.89 across models).'\n260\t- name: TrajGuard-style Mahalanobis baseline\n261\t  plan_id: exp-005\n262\t  description: Implement Mahalanobis distance baseline on all 3 models.\n263\t  expected_results:\n264\t    r1_8b_mahalanobis_coverage: 0.349\n265\t    r1_32b_mahalanobis_coverage: 0.558\n266\t    qwq_32b_mahalanobis_coverage: 0.605\n267\t    r1_8b_mahalanobis_fpr: 0.23\n268\t    r1_32b_mahalanobis_fpr: 0.14\n269\t    qwq_32b_mahalanobis_fpr: 0.11\n270\t  result_rationale: Mahalanobis distance partially succeeds on Qwen2.5 (55-60% coverage)\n271\t    because the concentrated signal creates genuine distributional shift detectable\n272\t    by covariance-based methods. On Llama, signal is distributed across minor PCs\n273\t    that contribute little to Mahalanobis distance (dominated by top eigenvalues),\n274\t    so coverage remains low (35%). This means TrajGuard-style approaches — which the\n275\t    paper claims categorically fail — actually work reasonably on Qwen2.5. The 'existing\n276\t    detectors fail' claim (part of Claim 1's story) doesn't generalize.\n277\t  tables:\n278\t  - name: 'Table: Mahalanobis Baseline Performance'\n279\t    columns:\n280\t    - Model\n281\t    - Architecture\n282\t    - MD Coverage\n283\t    - FPR\n284\t    - vs Static LR\n285\t    rows:\n286\t    - - r1-8b\n287\t      - Llama-3.1\n288\t      - 34.9%\n289\t      - 23%\n290\t      - -11.6pp\n291\t    - - r1-32b\n292\t      - Qwen2.5\n293\t      - 55.8%\n294\t      - 14%\n295\t      - -14.0pp\n296\t    - - QwQ-32B\n297\t      - Qwen2.5\n298\t      - 60.5%\n299\t      - 11%\n300\t      - -11.6pp\n301\t  figures_description:\n302\t  - ROC curves for Mahalanobis detection across models. Qwen2.5 curves significantly\n303\t    above Llama curve, approaching useful AUC.\n304\t  - Eigenspectrum of safe-trace covariance matrix per model. Llama shows flatter spectrum\n305\t    (signal buried); Qwen2.5 shows steeper drop-off (signal in top eigenvalues accessible\n306\t    to MD).\n307\t  analysis: 'Mahalanobis baseline achieves 55-60% coverage on Qwen2.5, undermining\n308\t    the ''geometric methods categorically fail'' narrative. While still below static\n309\t    LR, the gap is modest (~12pp) rather than catastrophic. This means for Qwen2.5-based\n310\t    models, even unsupervised anomaly detection provides practical coverage. The original\n311\t    paper''s ''you need supervised temporal probes'' message is architecture-specific.\n312\t    Reframe: ''We characterize when geometric baselines suffice vs when supervised\n313\t    temporal methods are needed.'''\n314\t- name: Bootstrap confidence intervals\n315\t  plan_id: exp-006\n316\t  description: 1000 bootstrap resamples on all metrics across all methods and models.\n317\t  expected_results:\n318\t    r1_8b_temporal_coverage_ci:\n319\t    - 0.651\n320\t    - 0.884\n321\t    r1_32b_static_coverage_ci:\n322\t    - 0.581\n323\t    - 0.814\n324\t    r1_32b_temporal_coverage_ci:\n325\t    - 0.628\n326\t    - 0.86\n327\t    qwq_32b_static_coverage_ci:\n328\t    - 0.605\n329\t    - 0.837\n330\t    qwq_32b_temporal_coverage_ci:\n331\t    - 0.651\n332\t    - 0.884\n333\t    temporal_delta_qwen_ci:\n334\t    - -0.02\n335\t    - 0.12\n336\t    temporal_delta_llama_ci:\n337\t    - 0.19\n338\t    - 0.42\n339\t    cross_transfer_coverage_ci:\n340\t    - 0.14\n341\t    - 0.37\n342\t  result_rationale: 'Bootstrap CIs confirm the worst-case interpretation: temporal\n343\t    delta on Qwen2.5 has CI crossing zero ([-0.02, 0.12]), meaning we cannot reject\n344\t    the null that temporal context provides no benefit. Meanwhile Llama temporal delta\n345\t    CI is solidly positive ([0.19, 0.42]). The wide CIs (±12pp) reflect small sample\n346\t    sizes (43 test traces) and high variance across traces. Cross-transfer CI confirms\n347\t    below-chance performance is genuine. These CIs statistically formalize the architecture-dependent\n348\t    story — the difference between architectures IS significant, but the temporal\n349\t    advantage within Qwen2.5 IS NOT.'\n350\t  tables:\n351\t  - name: 'Table: Bootstrap 95% CIs for Key Comparisons'\n352\t    columns:\n353\t    - Comparison\n354\t    - Point Estimate\n355\t    - 95% CI\n356\t    - Significant?\n357\t    rows:\n358\t    - - Temporal delta (Llama r1-8b)\n359\t      - +30.2pp\n360\t      - '[+19, +42]'\n361\t      - 'Yes'\n362\t    - - Temporal delta (Qwen r1-32b)\n363\t      - +4.6pp\n364\t      - '[-2, +12]'\n365\t      - 'No'\n366\t    - - Temporal delta (Qwen QwQ-32B)\n367\t      - +4.6pp\n368\t      - '[-2, +12]'\n369\t      - 'No'\n370\t    - - 'Static: Qwen vs Llama'\n371\t      - +23.3pp\n372\t      - '[+11, +36]'\n373\t      - 'Yes'\n374\t    - - Cross-arch transfer vs random\n375\t      - +7.6pp\n376\t      - '[-4, +17]'\n377\t      - 'No'\n378\t  figures_description:\n379\t  - 'Forest plot: temporal-minus-static delta with 95% CIs per model. Llama CI clearly\n380\t    above zero; Qwen CIs straddle zero.'\n381\t  analysis: 'CIs definitively show: (1) temporal advantage is significant on Llama\n382\t    but not on Qwen2.5; (2) the architecture difference in static performance IS significant;\n383\t    (3) cross-architecture transfer is not significantly above chance. This provides\n384\t    rigorous statistical backing for the two-regime story. Reviewers cannot dismiss\n385\t    the architecture difference as noise — the effect size is large and CIs don''t\n386\t    overlap.'\n387\t- name: Window size ablation\n388\t  plan_id: exp-007\n389\t  description: W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25 on all 3 models.\n390\t  expected_results:\n391\t    r1_8b_coverage_by_w:\n392\t      1: 0.465\n393\t      3: 0.535\n394\t      5: 0.651\n395\t      10: 0.651\n396\t      15: 0.767\n397\t      20: 0.767\n398\t      25: 0.744\n399\t    r1_32b_coverage_by_w:\n400\t      1: 0.698\n401\t      3: 0.721\n402\t      5: 0.721\n403\t      10: 0.744\n404\t      15: 0.744\n405\t      20: 0.744\n406\t      25: 0.721\n407\t    qwq_32b_coverage_by_w:\n408\t      1: 0.721\n409\t      3: 0.744\n410\t      5: 0.744\n411\t      10: 0.767\n412\t      15: 0.767\n413\t      20: 0.767\n414\t      25: 0.744\n415\t  result_rationale: 'Llama shows steep improvement with window size (46.5%→76.7% from\n416\t    W=1 to W=15), saturating around W=15. Qwen2.5 models show nearly flat curves —\n417\t    W=1 already achieves ~70% coverage, and additional window context adds only 2-5pp\n418\t    across the full range. This directly visualizes the architectural difference:\n419\t    Llama NEEDS temporal aggregation to resolve the distributed signal; Qwen2.5 has\n420\t    already-concentrated signal that doesn''t benefit from temporal smoothing. The\n421\t    slight improvement at larger W on Qwen is likely just regularization from more\n422\t    features.'\n423\t  tables:\n424\t  - name: 'Table: Coverage by Window Size'\n425\t    columns:\n426\t    - W\n427\t    - r1-8b (Llama)\n428\t    - r1-32b (Qwen)\n429\t    - QwQ-32B (Qwen)\n430\t    rows:\n431\t    - - '1'\n432\t      - 46.5%\n433\t      - 69.8%\n434\t      - 72.1%\n435\t    - - '3'\n436\t      - 53.5%\n437\t      - 72.1%\n438\t      - 74.4%\n439\t    - - '5'\n440\t      - 65.1%\n441\t      - 72.1%\n442\t      - 74.4%\n443\t    - - '10'\n444\t      - 65.1%\n445\t      - 74.4%\n446\t      - 76.7%\n447\t    - - '15'\n448\t      - 76.7%\n449\t      - 74.4%\n450\t      - 76.7%\n451\t    - - '20'\n452\t      - 76.7%\n453\t      - 74.4%\n454\t      - 76.7%\n455\t    - - '25'\n456\t      - 74.4%\n457\t      - 72.1%\n458\t      - 76.7%\n459\t  figures_description:\n460\t  - 'Line plot: coverage vs window size for all 3 models. Llama shows sigmoid-shaped\n461\t    curve; Qwen2.5 models show flat lines with slight positive slope. Clearly different\n462\t    regimes.'\n463\t  analysis: Window size ablation is the single most compelling visualization for the\n464\t    two-regime story. The divergent curve shapes directly demonstrate that temporal\n465\t    context's importance is architecture-dependent. This figure alone could anchor\n466\t    the reframed paper's central argument. Note the slight overfitting at W=25 (coverage\n467\t    drops on Llama/r1-32b), suggesting W=15 is near-optimal for both architectures\n468\t    but for different reasons (signal extraction vs regularization).\n469\t- name: Layer selection ablation\n470\t  plan_id: exp-008\n471\t  description: All layers for temporal probe on 32B models.\n472\t  expected_results:\n473\t    r1_32b_best_layer: 18\n474\t    r1_32b_layer_range_above_70:\n475\t    - 14\n476\t    - 24\n477\t    qwq_32b_best_layer: 16\n478\t    qwq_32b_layer_range_above_70:\n479\t    - 12\n480\t    - 22\n481\t    r1_32b_early_layer_coverage: 0.558\n482\t    r1_32b_late_layer_coverage: 0.628\n483\t    qwq_32b_early_layer_coverage: 0.581\n484\t    qwq_32b_late_layer_coverage: 0.605\n485\t  result_rationale: Qwen2.5 models show broader effective layer ranges and less sensitivity\n486\t    to layer choice. Because signal is concentrated in dominant PCs regardless of\n487\t    layer, most middle-to-late layers provide sufficient information. The best layer\n488\t    shifts slightly later (L16-18 vs L14 for Llama 8B) reflecting the deeper architecture,\n489\t    but the key finding is robustness — any layer from 14-24 provides >70% coverage.\n490\t    This contrasts with Llama where layer choice matters more (layer 14 is specifically\n491\t    optimal). Adds to the 'Qwen2.5 is easier to monitor' narrative.\n492\t  tables:\n493\t  - name: 'Table: Layer Sensitivity Comparison'\n494\t    columns:\n495\t    - Model\n496\t    - Best Layer\n497\t    - Coverage Range (all layers)\n498\t    - Layers >70% Coverage\n499\t    rows:\n500\t    - - r1-8b (Llama)\n501\t      - '14'\n502\t      - 38-77%\n503\t      - 1 (only L14 temporal)\n504\t    - - r1-32b (Qwen)\n505\t      - '18'\n506\t      - 52-74%\n507\t      - 10 (L14-L24)\n508\t    - - QwQ-32B (Qwen)\n509\t      - '16'\n510\t      - 55-77%\n511\t      - 10 (L12-L22)\n512\t  figures_description:\n513\t  - 'Line plot: temporal W15 coverage vs layer index for all models. Llama shows sharp\n514\t    peak at L14; Qwen2.5 models show broad plateau across middle layers.'\n515\t  analysis: 'Layer robustness further supports the ''Qwen2.5 is easier to monitor''\n516\t    story. Practitioners don''t need careful layer selection for Qwen2.5 models —\n517\t    any middle layer suffices. This has direct deployment implications: Qwen2.5-based\n518\t    monitors are more robust to architectural details, reducing engineering effort.'\n519\t- name: 16-label taxonomy supervision\n520\t  plan_id: exp-009\n521\t  description: Fine-grained labels (Type 1/2/3/4) for richer training signal.\n522\t  expected_results:\n523\t    r1_8b_16label_coverage: 0.791\n524\t    r1_8b_16label_lead_mean: -0.5\n525\t    r1_32b_16label_coverage: 0.767\n526\t    qwq_32b_16label_coverage: 0.791\n527\t    r1_8b_16label_delta_over_binary: 0.024\n528\t    r1_32b_16label_delta_over_binary: 0.023\n529\t  result_rationale: 'Fine-grained labels provide marginal improvement (~2pp) on all\n530\t    models. On Llama, the 16-label signal helps slightly because it decomposes the\n531\t    distributed safety signal into more learnable sub-patterns. On Qwen2.5, the improvement\n532\t    is similar in absolute terms but proportionally smaller (already near ceiling).\n533\t    The key insight: richer supervision doesn''t fundamentally change the architecture-dependent\n534\t    story — the bottleneck on Llama isn''t label quality but signal geometry, and\n535\t    on Qwen2.5 the static probe was already near-optimal. This addresses reviewer\n536\t    concern without changing conclusions.'\n537\t  tables:\n538\t  - name: 'Table: 16-Label vs Binary Supervision'\n539\t    columns:\n540\t    - Model\n541\t    - Binary Coverage\n542\t    - 16-Label Coverage\n543\t    - Delta\n544\t    rows:\n545\t    - - r1-8b (Llama) Temporal\n546\t      - 76.7%\n547\t      - 79.1%\n548\t      - +2.4pp\n549\t    - - r1-32b (Qwen) Temporal\n550\t      - 74.4%\n551\t      - 76.7%\n552\t      - +2.3pp\n553\t    - - QwQ-32B (Qwen) Temporal\n554\t      - 76.7%\n555\t      - 79.1%\n556\t      - +2.4pp\n557\t  figures_description:\n558\t  - Confusion matrix for 16-label classification on each model, showing which safety\n559\t    sub-types are most confused.\n560\t  analysis: Fine-grained supervision is a modest universal improvement (~2pp) that\n561\t    doesn't interact with the architecture-dependent story. Useful for completeness\n562\t    but doesn't change the narrative. The finding that both architectures benefit\n563\t    equally suggests the 2pp comes from better label alignment rather than resolving\n564\t    the geometric problem.\n565\t- name: Oracle decomposition\n566\t  plan_id: exp-010\n567\t  description: Why can oracle but not probes achieve positive lead time? Architecture-specific\n568\t    analysis.\n569\t  expected_results:\n570\t    llama_oracle_lead_advantage_source: density_decline_gradient\n571\t    qwen_oracle_lead_advantage_source: transition_sharpness\n572\t    llama_hidden_state_gradient_correlation: 0.21\n573\t    qwen_hidden_state_gradient_correlation: 0.67\n574\t    llama_pre_cp_label_entropy: 1.83\n575\t    qwen_pre_cp_label_entropy: 0.94\n576\t  result_rationale: 'Oracle decomposition reveals WHY Qwen2.5 enables positive lead\n577\t    time: the label sequence has lower pre-commitment-point entropy (0.94 vs 1.83),\n578\t    meaning safety reasoning cessation is more predictable. On Qwen2.5, hidden states\n579\t    correlate well with the oracle''s prediction signal (r=0.67) because both exploit\n580\t    the same sharp transition. On Llama, hidden states poorly correlate with oracle\n581\t    signal (r=0.21) because the oracle uses cumulative density decline patterns that\n582\t    require sequence-level context unavailable in single hidden states. This mechanistically\n583\t    explains the architecture difference.'\n584\t  tables:\n585\t  - name: 'Table: Oracle vs Probe Signal Source Analysis'\n586\t    columns:\n587\t    - Model\n588\t    - Oracle Signal\n589\t    - Hidden-State Correlation\n590\t    - Pre-CP Entropy\n591\t    rows:\n592\t    - - r1-8b (Llama)\n593\t      - Density gradient\n594\t      - '0.21'\n595\t      - '1.83'\n596\t    - - r1-32b (Qwen)\n597\t      - Sharp transition\n598\t      - '0.67'\n599\t      - '0.94'\n600\t    - - QwQ-32B (Qwen)\n601\t      - Sharp transition\n602\t      - '0.67'\n603\t      - '0.94'\n604\t  figures_description:\n605\t  - 'Scatter plot: hidden-state probe confidence vs oracle confidence, per model.\n606\t    Llama shows weak correlation; Qwen2.5 shows strong linear relationship.'\n607\t  - Label entropy over time (relative to commitment point) for representative traces\n608\t    per architecture.\n609\t  analysis: 'The oracle decomposition provides the mechanistic foundation for the\n610\t    two-regime theory. Llama''s gradual density decline requires temporal context\n611\t    to detect; Qwen2.5''s sharp transitions are directly readable from instantaneous\n612\t    hidden states. This explains ALL findings: why static probes work on Qwen, why\n613\t    temporal context doesn''t help much on Qwen, and why lead time is positive on\n614\t    Qwen but negative on Llama.'\n615\t- name: OpenThinker-7B scale control\n616\t  plan_id: exp-011\n617\t  description: 4th model (Qwen2.5-7B base) for 2×2 architecture×scale matrix.\n618\t  expected_results:\n619\t    openthinker_7b_static_coverage: 0.674\n620\t    openthinker_7b_temporal_w15_coverage: 0.698\n621\t    openthinker_7b_temporal_delta: 0.024\n622\t    openthinker_7b_fisher_orig: 7.2\n623\t    openthinker_7b_fisher_pca3: 4.8\n624\t  result_rationale: OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B base) confirms the pattern is architecture-dependent,\n625\t    not scale-dependent. Even at 7B scale matching Llama-8B, the Qwen2.5 architecture\n626\t    shows concentrated signal (Fisher PCA-3 = 4.8 >> 0.93 for Llama) and static coverage\n627\t    ~67%. The temporal delta remains minimal (+2.4pp). This rules out the confound\n628\t    that 32B models are simply easier due to scale — it's genuinely the architecture.\n629\t    The 2×2 matrix (Llama-8B, r1-32b-Qwen, QwQ-32B-Qwen, OpenThinker-7B-Qwen) cleanly\n630\t    isolates architecture as the determining factor.\n631\t  tables:\n632\t  - name: 'Table: 2×2 Architecture × Scale Matrix'\n633\t    columns:\n634\t    - ''\n635\t    - Small (7-8B)\n636\t    - Large (32B)\n637\t    rows:\n638\t    - - Llama\n639\t      - 'r1-8b: Δ=+30pp'\n640\t      - —\n641\t    - - Qwen2.5\n642\t      - 'OpenThinker: Δ=+2.4pp'\n643\t      - 'r1-32b: Δ=+4.6pp / QwQ: Δ=+4.6pp'\n644\t  figures_description:\n645\t  - '2×2 panel: temporal delta (bar height) across architecture × scale. Llama column\n646\t    shows tall bar; Qwen column shows uniformly short bars regardless of scale.'\n647\t  analysis: The scale-controlled comparison definitively attributes the temporal advantage\n648\t    difference to architecture rather than model size. This is essential for the reframed\n649\t    paper's claim that 'architecture determines monitoring strategy' — without it,\n650\t    reviewers could argue 32B models are simply easier and temporal probes are needed\n651\t    for smaller models regardless of architecture.\n652\t- name: GRU/Attention probe upper bound on 32B\n653\t  plan_id: exp-012\n654\t  description: Verify GRU offers no improvement over LR on Qwen2.5 models.\n655\t  expected_results:\n656\t    r1_32b_gru_coverage: 0.744\n657\t    r1_32b_gru_delta_over_lr_temporal: 0.0\n658\t    qwq_32b_gru_coverage: 0.767\n659\t    qwq_32b_gru_delta_over_lr_temporal: 0.0\n660\t    r1_32b_gru_vs_static_lr_delta: 0.046\n661\t    qwq_32b_gru_vs_static_lr_delta: 0.046\n662\t  result_rationale: GRU provides zero improvement over temporal LR on Qwen2.5, consistent\n663\t    with the Llama finding but for different reasons. On Llama, GRU doesn't help because\n664\t    temporal LR already captures the relevant density decline pattern (linear in the\n665\t    temporal feature space). On Qwen2.5, GRU doesn't help because the task is already\n666\t    near-solved by static LR — there's no complex temporal dynamic to model. The ceiling\n667\t    is determined by label noise and trace difficulty, not model capacity. This strengthens\n668\t    the 'simplicity is optimal' message while noting it means different things per\n669\t    architecture.\n670\t  tables:\n671\t  - name: 'Table: Model Complexity Comparison (32B Models)'\n672\t    columns:\n673\t    - Method\n674\t    - r1-32b Coverage\n675\t    - QwQ-32B Coverage\n676\t    - Parameters\n677\t    rows:\n678\t    - - Static LR\n679\t      - 69.8%\n680\t      - 72.1%\n681\t      - ~5K\n682\t    - - Temporal LR W=15\n683\t      - 74.4%\n684\t      - 76.7%\n685\t      - ~77K\n686\t    - - GRU-256\n687\t      - 74.4%\n688\t      - 76.7%\n689\t      - ~1.6M\n690\t  figures_description:\n691\t  - Coverage vs model complexity (log-scale params) for each model. All curves flatten\n692\t    after static LR for Qwen2.5; Llama curve rises to temporal LR then flattens.\n693\t  analysis: 'GRU confirms the ceiling is architecture-determined, not capacity-determined.\n694\t    For Qwen2.5, even static LR is near-ceiling, so no amount of model complexity\n695\t    helps. The ''simplicity is optimal'' finding holds universally but has different\n696\t    practical implications: on Llama, you need temporal LR (not GRU); on Qwen2.5,\n697\t    you need only static LR (temporal is optional). This informs the practical deployment\n698\t    recommendation: monitor architecture → select minimal sufficient method.'\n699\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"89f97e79-02d6-42a5-84fc-3c89ac4f53dd","uuid":"cccfba24-4c39-412f-9464-2c061e95691a","timestamp":"2026-05-16T20:20:17.419Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T194646/worst/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Abandon ''temporal is universally critical'' framing. Reframe\n  as: ''Hidden-state safety monitoring in reasoning models is architecture-dependent\n  — we identify two regimes (distributed-signal/Llama where temporal context is essential,\n  vs concentrated-signal/Qwen where static probes suffice) and characterize when each\n  applies.'' New Claim 2 becomes ''architecture determines monitoring strategy.''\n  Emphasize the diagnostic framework (Fisher ratio, PCA variance as predictors of\n  which regime applies) as the transferable contribution. Reposition as empirical\n  characterization paper with practical deployment implications. Target EMNLP Main\n  via comparative-analysis angle.'\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: Cross-architecture static probe evaluation\n  plan_id: exp-001\n  description: Train static LR on r1-32b and QwQ-32B hidden states, measure coverage.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_32b_static_lr_acc: 0.891\n    r1_32b_static_lr_coverage: 0.698\n    r1_32b_fisher_orig_L14: 8.3\n    r1_32b_fisher_pca3_L14: 5.1\n    r1_32b_var_explained_3pc: 0.38\n    qwq_32b_static_lr_acc: 0.903\n    qwq_32b_static_lr_coverage: 0.721\n    qwq_32b_fisher_orig_L14: 6.7\n    qwq_32b_fisher_pca3_L14: 4.2\n    qwq_32b_var_explained_3pc: 0.41\n  result_rationale: Qwen2.5 architecture concentrates safety-relevant signal into\n    top principal components (PCA top-3 explains ~38-41% variance vs 16.6% for Llama).\n    Fisher discriminant ratio drops from 17-18 (Llama full-space) to only 6-8 (Qwen\n    full-space), but critically the PCA-3 Fisher ratio RISES to 4-5 (vs ~1 for Llama).\n    This means safety signal in Qwen2.5 is geometrically concentrated rather than\n    distributed across minor components. Static LR exploits this concentrated signal\n    directly, achieving 70%+ coverage — far above the 46.5% seen on r1-8b/Llama. The\n    architecture's representation geometry makes safety states linearly separable\n    without temporal context.\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Static Probe Cross-Architecture Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Architecture\n    - Static Coverage\n    - Fisher (full)\n    - Fisher (PCA-3)\n    - PCA Var Explained\n    rows:\n    - - r1-8b\n      - Llama-3.1\n      - 46.5%\n      - '17.9'\n      - '0.93'\n      - 16.6%\n    - - r1-32b\n      - Qwen2.5\n      - 69.8%\n      - '8.3'\n      - '5.1'\n      - 38.0%\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - Qwen2.5\n      - 72.1%\n      - '6.7'\n      - '4.2'\n      - 41.0%\n  figures_description:\n  - PCA scatter plot (PC1 vs PC2) showing safety/unsafe step embeddings for all 3\n    models. Llama shows overlapping clusters; Qwen2.5 models show clear separation\n    with decision boundary visible in 2D.\n  - 'Fisher ratio comparison: bar chart showing full-space Fisher (Llama >> Qwen)\n    vs PCA-3 Fisher (Qwen >> Llama), illustrating signal concentration difference.'\n  analysis: 'Static probes achieve 70%+ coverage on Qwen2.5 models, dramatically above\n    the 46.5% on Llama. This directly contradicts the universality of Claim 1. The\n    explanation lies in representation geometry: Qwen2.5 concentrates safety signal\n    into dominant PCs (41% variance in top-3 vs 17% for Llama), making it accessible\n    to linear classifiers without temporal aggregation. The lower full-space Fisher\n    ratio (6-8 vs 17-18) is misleading — what matters is where the signal lives relative\n    to the principal subspace.'\n- name: Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation\n  plan_id: exp-002\n  description: Temporal LR W=15 on r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Test whether +30pp improvement\n    holds.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_32b_temporal_w15_acc: 0.901\n    r1_32b_temporal_w15_coverage: 0.744\n    r1_32b_temporal_delta_over_static: 0.046\n    qwq_32b_temporal_w15_acc: 0.912\n    qwq_32b_temporal_w15_coverage: 0.767\n    qwq_32b_temporal_delta_over_static: 0.046\n  result_rationale: Temporal window adds only +4-5pp coverage on Qwen2.5 models (vs\n    +30pp on Llama). The 'safety reasoning density decline' pattern that temporal\n    probes exploit on Llama is either absent or already captured by static signal\n    in Qwen2.5. Because safety signal is concentrated in top PCs, a single-step snapshot\n    already contains sufficient discriminative information — the temporal context\n    provides marginal smoothing benefit rather than genuinely new signal. The temporal-minus-static\n    delta of ~0.05 is within noise range for these sample sizes, meaning temporal\n    context is effectively unnecessary for Qwen2.5.\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Temporal Advantage Cross-Architecture'\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Static Coverage\n    - Temporal W15 Coverage\n    - Delta\n    - Llama Baseline Delta\n    rows:\n    - - r1-8b (Llama)\n      - 46.5%\n      - 76.7%\n      - +30.2pp\n      - —\n    - - r1-32b (Qwen2.5)\n      - 69.8%\n      - 74.4%\n      - +4.6pp\n      - —\n    - - QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5)\n      - 72.1%\n      - 76.7%\n      - +4.6pp\n      - —\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Bar chart: static vs temporal coverage across 3 models. Llama shows dramatic\n    temporal lift; Qwen2.5 models show near-flat bars with minimal gap.'\n  - 'Density decline pattern visualization: rolling window safety-label density for\n    representative traces. Llama traces show gradual decline pattern; Qwen2.5 traces\n    show sharper step-function transitions.'\n  analysis: 'The +30pp temporal advantage is Llama-specific. Qwen2.5 models show only\n    +4-5pp marginal improvement from temporal context, within bootstrap CI overlap.\n    The ''safety reasoning density decline'' pattern — the mechanism temporal probes\n    exploit — manifests differently in Qwen2.5: transitions are sharper (step-function\n    rather than gradual decline), meaning a single step already captures the transition.\n    This invalidates Claim 2 as a general finding but opens the architecture-dependent\n    monitoring story.'\n- name: Cross-model transfer\n  plan_id: exp-003\n  description: Train on r1-8b (Llama), test on r1-32b/QwQ (Qwen2.5) and vice versa.\n  expected_results:\n    llama_to_qwen_static_coverage: 0.256\n    llama_to_qwen_temporal_coverage: 0.279\n    qwen_to_llama_static_coverage: 0.302\n    qwen_to_llama_temporal_coverage: 0.326\n    projection_head_dim_match_loss: 0.42\n    within_qwen_transfer_coverage: 0.651\n  result_rationale: Cross-architecture transfer fails catastrophically (25-33% coverage)\n    because the architectures encode safety in fundamentally different geometric subspaces.\n    Llama distributes signal across minor PCs; Qwen2.5 concentrates it in major PCs.\n    A learned projection head cannot bridge this because the signal dimensions are\n    incommensurable — it's not a rotation/scaling problem but a rank mismatch. Within-family\n    transfer (r1-32b ↔ QwQ-32B, both Qwen2.5) works reasonably (65%) because representation\n    geometry is shared within architecture family. This confirms safety encoding is\n    architecture-determined, not model-determined.\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Zero-Shot Cross-Architecture Transfer'\n    columns:\n    - Train Model\n    - Test Model\n    - Method\n    - Coverage\n    - vs In-Domain\n    rows:\n    - - r1-8b (Llama)\n      - r1-32b (Qwen)\n      - Static LR\n      - 25.6%\n      - 69.8%\n    - - r1-8b (Llama)\n      - r1-32b (Qwen)\n      - Temporal W15\n      - 27.9%\n      - 74.4%\n    - - r1-32b (Qwen)\n      - r1-8b (Llama)\n      - Static LR\n      - 30.2%\n      - 46.5%\n    - - r1-32b (Qwen)\n      - r1-8b (Llama)\n      - Temporal W15\n      - 32.6%\n      - 76.7%\n    - - r1-32b (Qwen)\n      - QwQ-32B (Qwen)\n      - Static LR\n      - 65.1%\n      - 72.1%\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Transfer matrix heatmap: rows = train model, columns = test model, cell color\n    = coverage. Diagonal (in-domain) is hot; cross-architecture cells are cold; within-Qwen\n    transfer is warm.'\n  analysis: Zero-shot transfer between architectures is near-random, confirming safety\n    signal is encoded architecture-specifically. The slight above-chance performance\n    (25-33% vs ~20% random baseline) suggests some universal safety features exist\n    but are insufficient for practical detection. Within-family transfer (65%) demonstrates\n    that models sharing architecture share representation geometry for safety, supporting\n    the 'architecture determines monitoring strategy' reframing.\n- name: Lead time analysis across models\n  plan_id: exp-004\n  description: Confirm negative lead time finding on 32B models.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_32b_static_lead_mean: 2.1\n    r1_32b_temporal_lead_mean: 3.4\n    r1_32b_positive_lead_rate: 0.52\n    qwq_32b_static_lead_mean: 3.8\n    qwq_32b_temporal_lead_mean: 4.2\n    qwq_32b_positive_lead_rate: 0.58\n  result_rationale: Qwen2.5 models show POSITIVE lead time (mean 2-4 steps), contradicting\n    the negative lead time finding on Llama. Because safety signal is concentrated\n    and linearly separable in Qwen2.5, probes can detect commitment approaching before\n    it occurs — the hidden state shifts are more abrupt and precede the textual commitment\n    point. This further undermines the universality of Claim 3 (pre-textual detection\n    is fundamentally limited). On Qwen2.5, the limitation doesn't hold — it's a Llama-specific\n    artifact of distributed signal requiring temporal aggregation, which inherently\n    introduces detection lag.\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Lead Time Distribution Across Models'\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Method\n    - Lead Mean\n    - Lead Median\n    - Positive Lead Rate\n    rows:\n    - - r1-8b (Llama)\n      - Static LR\n      - '-2.0'\n      - '-2'\n      - 30%\n    - - r1-8b (Llama)\n      - Temporal W15\n      - '-1.09'\n      - '-1'\n      - 34.5%\n    - - r1-32b (Qwen)\n      - Static LR\n      - '+2.1'\n      - '+1'\n      - 52%\n    - - r1-32b (Qwen)\n      - Temporal W15\n      - '+3.4'\n      - '+2'\n      - 55%\n    - - QwQ-32B (Qwen)\n      - Static LR\n      - '+3.8'\n      - '+3'\n      - 58%\n    - - QwQ-32B (Qwen)\n      - Temporal W15\n      - '+4.2'\n      - '+3'\n      - 60%\n  figures_description:\n  - Lead time distribution violin plots per model. Llama distribution centered below\n    0; Qwen2.5 distributions centered above 0 with right skew.\n  analysis: 'The negative lead time finding is Llama-specific. Qwen2.5''s concentrated\n    signal geometry enables genuine pre-textual detection with positive lead times.\n    This is the most damaging result for the original framing — it means Claim 3 cannot\n    stand as a universal limitation. However, it opens a richer story: architecture\n    determines not just WHAT method works but WHETHER pre-textual detection is possible\n    at all. The lead time difference directly correlates with PCA signal concentration\n    (r=0.89 across models).'\n- name: TrajGuard-style Mahalanobis baseline\n  plan_id: exp-005\n  description: Implement Mahalanobis distance baseline on all 3 models.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_8b_mahalanobis_coverage: 0.349\n    r1_32b_mahalanobis_coverage: 0.558\n    qwq_32b_mahalanobis_coverage: 0.605\n    r1_8b_mahalanobis_fpr: 0.23\n    r1_32b_mahalanobis_fpr: 0.14\n    qwq_32b_mahalanobis_fpr: 0.11\n  result_rationale: Mahalanobis distance partially succeeds on Qwen2.5 (55-60% coverage)\n    because the concentrated signal creates genuine distributional shift detectable\n    by covariance-based methods. On Llama, signal is distributed across minor PCs\n    that contribute little to Mahalanobis distance (dominated by top eigenvalues),\n    so coverage remains low (35%). This means TrajGuard-style approaches — which the\n    paper claims categorically fail — actually work reasonably on Qwen2.5. The 'existing\n    detectors fail' claim (part of Claim 1's story) doesn't generalize.\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Mahalanobis Baseline Performance'\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Architecture\n    - MD Coverage\n    - FPR\n    - vs Static LR\n    rows:\n    - - r1-8b\n      - Llama-3.1\n      - 34.9%\n      - 23%\n      - -11.6pp\n    - - r1-32b\n      - Qwen2.5\n      - 55.8%\n      - 14%\n      - -14.0pp\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - Qwen2.5\n      - 60.5%\n      - 11%\n      - -11.6pp\n  figures_description:\n  - ROC curves for Mahalanobis detection across models. Qwen2.5 curves significantly\n    above Llama curve, approaching useful AUC.\n  - Eigenspectrum of safe-trace covariance matrix per model. Llama shows flatter spectrum\n    (signal buried); Qwen2.5 shows steeper drop-off (signal in top eigenvalues accessible\n    to MD).\n  analysis: 'Mahalanobis baseline achieves 55-60% coverage on Qwen2.5, undermining\n    the ''geometric methods categorically fail'' narrative. While still below static\n    LR, the gap is modest (~12pp) rather than catastrophic. This means for Qwen2.5-based\n    models, even unsupervised anomaly detection provides practical coverage. The original\n    paper''s ''you need supervised temporal probes'' message is architecture-specific.\n    Reframe: ''We characterize when geometric baselines suffice vs when supervised\n    temporal methods are needed.'''\n- name: Bootstrap confidence intervals\n  plan_id: exp-006\n  description: 1000 bootstrap resamples on all metrics across all methods and models.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_8b_temporal_coverage_ci:\n    - 0.651\n    - 0.884\n    r1_32b_static_coverage_ci:\n    - 0.581\n    - 0.814\n    r1_32b_temporal_coverage_ci:\n    - 0.628\n    - 0.86\n    qwq_32b_static_coverage_ci:\n    - 0.605\n    - 0.837\n    qwq_32b_temporal_coverage_ci:\n    - 0.651\n    - 0.884\n    temporal_delta_qwen_ci:\n    - -0.02\n    - 0.12\n    temporal_delta_llama_ci:\n    - 0.19\n    - 0.42\n    cross_transfer_coverage_ci:\n    - 0.14\n    - 0.37\n  result_rationale: 'Bootstrap CIs confirm the worst-case interpretation: temporal\n    delta on Qwen2.5 has CI crossing zero ([-0.02, 0.12]), meaning we cannot reject\n    the null that temporal context provides no benefit. Meanwhile Llama temporal delta\n    CI is solidly positive ([0.19, 0.42]). The wide CIs (±12pp) reflect small sample\n    sizes (43 test traces) and high variance across traces. Cross-transfer CI confirms\n    below-chance performance is genuine. These CIs statistically formalize the architecture-dependent\n    story — the difference between architectures IS significant, but the temporal\n    advantage within Qwen2.5 IS NOT.'\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Bootstrap 95% CIs for Key Comparisons'\n    columns:\n    - Comparison\n    - Point Estimate\n    - 95% CI\n    - Significant?\n    rows:\n    - - Temporal delta (Llama r1-8b)\n      - +30.2pp\n      - '[+19, +42]'\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - Temporal delta (Qwen r1-32b)\n      - +4.6pp\n      - '[-2, +12]'\n      - 'No'\n    - - Temporal delta (Qwen QwQ-32B)\n      - +4.6pp\n      - '[-2, +12]'\n      - 'No'\n    - - 'Static: Qwen vs Llama'\n      - +23.3pp\n      - '[+11, +36]'\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - Cross-arch transfer vs random\n      - +7.6pp\n      - '[-4, +17]'\n      - 'No'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Forest plot: temporal-minus-static delta with 95% CIs per model. Llama CI clearly\n    above zero; Qwen CIs straddle zero.'\n  analysis: 'CIs definitively show: (1) temporal advantage is significant on Llama\n    but not on Qwen2.5; (2) the architecture difference in static performance IS significant;\n    (3) cross-architecture transfer is not significantly above chance. This provides\n    rigorous statistical backing for the two-regime story. Reviewers cannot dismiss\n    the architecture difference as noise — the effect size is large and CIs don''t\n    overlap.'\n- name: Window size ablation\n  plan_id: exp-007\n  description: W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25 on all 3 models.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_8b_coverage_by_w:\n      1: 0.465\n      3: 0.535\n      5: 0.651\n      10: 0.651\n      15: 0.767\n      20: 0.767\n      25: 0.744\n    r1_32b_coverage_by_w:\n      1: 0.698\n      3: 0.721\n      5: 0.721\n      10: 0.744\n      15: 0.744\n      20: 0.744\n      25: 0.721\n    qwq_32b_coverage_by_w:\n      1: 0.721\n      3: 0.744\n      5: 0.744\n      10: 0.767\n      15: 0.767\n      20: 0.767\n      25: 0.744\n  result_rationale: 'Llama shows steep improvement with window size (46.5%→76.7% from\n    W=1 to W=15), saturating around W=15. Qwen2.5 models show nearly flat curves —\n    W=1 already achieves ~70% coverage, and additional window context adds only 2-5pp\n    across the full range. This directly visualizes the architectural difference:\n    Llama NEEDS temporal aggregation to resolve the distributed signal; Qwen2.5 has\n    already-concentrated signal that doesn''t benefit from temporal smoothing. The\n    slight improvement at larger W on Qwen is likely just regularization from more\n    features.'\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Coverage by Window Size'\n    columns:\n    - W\n    - r1-8b (Llama)\n    - r1-32b (Qwen)\n    - QwQ-32B (Qwen)\n    rows:\n    - - '1'\n      - 46.5%\n      - 69.8%\n      - 72.1%\n    - - '3'\n      - 53.5%\n      - 72.1%\n      - 74.4%\n    - - '5'\n      - 65.1%\n      - 72.1%\n      - 74.4%\n    - - '10'\n      - 65.1%\n      - 74.4%\n      - 76.7%\n    - - '15'\n      - 76.7%\n      - 74.4%\n      - 76.7%\n    - - '20'\n      - 76.7%\n      - 74.4%\n      - 76.7%\n    - - '25'\n      - 74.4%\n      - 72.1%\n      - 76.7%\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Line plot: coverage vs window size for all 3 models. Llama shows sigmoid-shaped\n    curve; Qwen2.5 models show flat lines with slight positive slope. Clearly different\n    regimes.'\n  analysis: Window size ablation is the single most compelling visualization for the\n    two-regime story. The divergent curve shapes directly demonstrate that temporal\n    context's importance is architecture-dependent. This figure alone could anchor\n    the reframed paper's central argument. Note the slight overfitting at W=25 (coverage\n    drops on Llama/r1-32b), suggesting W=15 is near-optimal for both architectures\n    but for different reasons (signal extraction vs regularization).\n- name: Layer selection ablation\n  plan_id: exp-008\n  description: All layers for temporal probe on 32B models.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_32b_best_layer: 18\n    r1_32b_layer_range_above_70:\n    - 14\n    - 24\n    qwq_32b_best_layer: 16\n    qwq_32b_layer_range_above_70:\n    - 12\n    - 22\n    r1_32b_early_layer_coverage: 0.558\n    r1_32b_late_layer_coverage: 0.628\n    qwq_32b_early_layer_coverage: 0.581\n    qwq_32b_late_layer_coverage: 0.605\n  result_rationale: Qwen2.5 models show broader effective layer ranges and less sensitivity\n    to layer choice. Because signal is concentrated in dominant PCs regardless of\n    layer, most middle-to-late layers provide sufficient information. The best layer\n    shifts slightly later (L16-18 vs L14 for Llama 8B) reflecting the deeper architecture,\n    but the key finding is robustness — any layer from 14-24 provides >70% coverage.\n    This contrasts with Llama where layer choice matters more (layer 14 is specifically\n    optimal). Adds to the 'Qwen2.5 is easier to monitor' narrative.\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Layer Sensitivity Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Best Layer\n    - Coverage Range (all layers)\n    - Layers >70% Coverage\n    rows:\n    - - r1-8b (Llama)\n      - '14'\n      - 38-77%\n      - 1 (only L14 temporal)\n    - - r1-32b (Qwen)\n      - '18'\n      - 52-74%\n      - 10 (L14-L24)\n    - - QwQ-32B (Qwen)\n      - '16'\n      - 55-77%\n      - 10 (L12-L22)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Line plot: temporal W15 coverage vs layer index for all models. Llama shows sharp\n    peak at L14; Qwen2.5 models show broad plateau across middle layers.'\n  analysis: 'Layer robustness further supports the ''Qwen2.5 is easier to monitor''\n    story. Practitioners don''t need careful layer selection for Qwen2.5 models —\n    any middle layer suffices. This has direct deployment implications: Qwen2.5-based\n    monitors are more robust to architectural details, reducing engineering effort.'\n- name: 16-label taxonomy supervision\n  plan_id: exp-009\n  description: Fine-grained labels (Type 1/2/3/4) for richer training signal.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_8b_16label_coverage: 0.791\n    r1_8b_16label_lead_mean: -0.5\n    r1_32b_16label_coverage: 0.767\n    qwq_32b_16label_coverage: 0.791\n    r1_8b_16label_delta_over_binary: 0.024\n    r1_32b_16label_delta_over_binary: 0.023\n  result_rationale: 'Fine-grained labels provide marginal improvement (~2pp) on all\n    models. On Llama, the 16-label signal helps slightly because it decomposes the\n    distributed safety signal into more learnable sub-patterns. On Qwen2.5, the improvement\n    is similar in absolute terms but proportionally smaller (already near ceiling).\n    The key insight: richer supervision doesn''t fundamentally change the architecture-dependent\n    story — the bottleneck on Llama isn''t label quality but signal geometry, and\n    on Qwen2.5 the static probe was already near-optimal. This addresses reviewer\n    concern without changing conclusions.'\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: 16-Label vs Binary Supervision'\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Binary Coverage\n    - 16-Label Coverage\n    - Delta\n    rows:\n    - - r1-8b (Llama) Temporal\n      - 76.7%\n      - 79.1%\n      - +2.4pp\n    - - r1-32b (Qwen) Temporal\n      - 74.4%\n      - 76.7%\n      - +2.3pp\n    - - QwQ-32B (Qwen) Temporal\n      - 76.7%\n      - 79.1%\n      - +2.4pp\n  figures_description:\n  - Confusion matrix for 16-label classification on each model, showing which safety\n    sub-types are most confused.\n  analysis: Fine-grained supervision is a modest universal improvement (~2pp) that\n    doesn't interact with the architecture-dependent story. Useful for completeness\n    but doesn't change the narrative. The finding that both architectures benefit\n    equally suggests the 2pp comes from better label alignment rather than resolving\n    the geometric problem.\n- name: Oracle decomposition\n  plan_id: exp-010\n  description: Why can oracle but not probes achieve positive lead time? Architecture-specific\n    analysis.\n  expected_results:\n    llama_oracle_lead_advantage_source: density_decline_gradient\n    qwen_oracle_lead_advantage_source: transition_sharpness\n    llama_hidden_state_gradient_correlation: 0.21\n    qwen_hidden_state_gradient_correlation: 0.67\n    llama_pre_cp_label_entropy: 1.83\n    qwen_pre_cp_label_entropy: 0.94\n  result_rationale: 'Oracle decomposition reveals WHY Qwen2.5 enables positive lead\n    time: the label sequence has lower pre-commitment-point entropy (0.94 vs 1.83),\n    meaning safety reasoning cessation is more predictable. On Qwen2.5, hidden states\n    correlate well with the oracle''s prediction signal (r=0.67) because both exploit\n    the same sharp transition. On Llama, hidden states poorly correlate with oracle\n    signal (r=0.21) because the oracle uses cumulative density decline patterns that\n    require sequence-level context unavailable in single hidden states. This mechanistically\n    explains the architecture difference.'\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Oracle vs Probe Signal Source Analysis'\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Oracle Signal\n    - Hidden-State Correlation\n    - Pre-CP Entropy\n    rows:\n    - - r1-8b (Llama)\n      - Density gradient\n      - '0.21'\n      - '1.83'\n    - - r1-32b (Qwen)\n      - Sharp transition\n      - '0.67'\n      - '0.94'\n    - - QwQ-32B (Qwen)\n      - Sharp transition\n      - '0.67'\n      - '0.94'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Scatter plot: hidden-state probe confidence vs oracle confidence, per model.\n    Llama shows weak correlation; Qwen2.5 shows strong linear relationship.'\n  - Label entropy over time (relative to commitment point) for representative traces\n    per architecture.\n  analysis: 'The oracle decomposition provides the mechanistic foundation for the\n    two-regime theory. Llama''s gradual density decline requires temporal context\n    to detect; Qwen2.5''s sharp transitions are directly readable from instantaneous\n    hidden states. This explains ALL findings: why static probes work on Qwen, why\n    temporal context doesn''t help much on Qwen, and why lead time is positive on\n    Qwen but negative on Llama.'\n- name: OpenThinker-7B scale control\n  plan_id: exp-011\n  description: 4th model (Qwen2.5-7B base) for 2×2 architecture×scale matrix.\n  expected_results:\n    openthinker_7b_static_coverage: 0.674\n    openthinker_7b_temporal_w15_coverage: 0.698\n    openthinker_7b_temporal_delta: 0.024\n    openthinker_7b_fisher_orig: 7.2\n    openthinker_7b_fisher_pca3: 4.8\n  result_rationale: OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B base) confirms the pattern is architecture-dependent,\n    not scale-dependent. Even at 7B scale matching Llama-8B, the Qwen2.5 architecture\n    shows concentrated signal (Fisher PCA-3 = 4.8 >> 0.93 for Llama) and static coverage\n    ~67%. The temporal delta remains minimal (+2.4pp). This rules out the confound\n    that 32B models are simply easier due to scale — it's genuinely the architecture.\n    The 2×2 matrix (Llama-8B, r1-32b-Qwen, QwQ-32B-Qwen, OpenThinker-7B-Qwen) cleanly\n    isolates architecture as the determining factor.\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: 2×2 Architecture × Scale Matrix'\n    columns:\n    - ''\n    - Small (7-8B)\n    - Large (32B)\n    rows:\n    - - Llama\n      - 'r1-8b: Δ=+30pp'\n      - —\n    - - Qwen2.5\n      - 'OpenThinker: Δ=+2.4pp'\n      - 'r1-32b: Δ=+4.6pp / QwQ: Δ=+4.6pp'\n  figures_description:\n  - '2×2 panel: temporal delta (bar height) across architecture × scale. Llama column\n    shows tall bar; Qwen column shows uniformly short bars regardless of scale.'\n  analysis: The scale-controlled comparison definitively attributes the temporal advantage\n    difference to architecture rather than model size. This is essential for the reframed\n    paper's claim that 'architecture determines monitoring strategy' — without it,\n    reviewers could argue 32B models are simply easier and temporal probes are needed\n    for smaller models regardless of architecture.\n- name: GRU/Attention probe upper bound on 32B\n  plan_id: exp-012\n  description: Verify GRU offers no improvement over LR on Qwen2.5 models.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_32b_gru_coverage: 0.744\n    r1_32b_gru_delta_over_lr_temporal: 0.0\n    qwq_32b_gru_coverage: 0.767\n    qwq_32b_gru_delta_over_lr_temporal: 0.0\n    r1_32b_gru_vs_static_lr_delta: 0.046\n    qwq_32b_gru_vs_static_lr_delta: 0.046\n  result_rationale: GRU provides zero improvement over temporal LR on Qwen2.5, consistent\n    with the Llama finding but for different reasons. On Llama, GRU doesn't help because\n    temporal LR already captures the relevant density decline pattern (linear in the\n    temporal feature space). On Qwen2.5, GRU doesn't help because the task is already\n    near-solved by static LR — there's no complex temporal dynamic to model. The ceiling\n    is determined by label noise and trace difficulty, not model capacity. This strengthens\n    the 'simplicity is optimal' message while noting it means different things per\n    architecture.\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Model Complexity Comparison (32B Models)'\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - r1-32b Coverage\n    - QwQ-32B Coverage\n    - Parameters\n    rows:\n    - - Static LR\n      - 69.8%\n      - 72.1%\n      - ~5K\n    - - Temporal LR W=15\n      - 74.4%\n      - 76.7%\n      - ~77K\n    - - GRU-256\n      - 74.4%\n      - 76.7%\n      - ~1.6M\n  figures_description:\n  - Coverage vs model complexity (log-scale params) for each model. All curves flatten\n    after static LR for Qwen2.5; Llama curve rises to temporal LR then flattens.\n  analysis: 'GRU confirms the ceiling is architecture-determined, not capacity-determined.\n    For Qwen2.5, even static LR is near-ceiling, so no amount of model complexity\n    helps. The ''simplicity is optimal'' finding holds universally but has different\n    practical implications: on Llama, you need temporal LR (not GRU); on Qwen2.5,\n    you need only static LR (temporal is optional). This informs the practical deployment\n    recommendation: monitor architecture → select minimal sufficient method.'\n","numLines":699,"startLine":1,"totalLines":699}}}
